Mini Planes 2.1.1

The domain types Airport, Airplane, AirplaneType, Passenger, Shape, Line,
Point, GameData, Updater and AirportSupplier evolve the supplied Java
prototype. Its presenter/editor design has been refactored into a ViewModel
with no reference back to the window; the simulation has no JavaFX imports.

Country outlines: Natural Earth, 1:50m Admin 0 Countries, public domain.
https://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/50m-cultural-vectors/
https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/
Input: ne_50m_admin_0_countries.geojson from
https://github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector
Downloaded 2026-11-19; clipped and simplified for two game regions.
Natural Earth uses de facto boundaries. These maps are for gameplay.

Airport coordinates, names and IATA codes: OurAirports, public domain.
https://ourairports.com/data/
https://davidmegginson.github.io/ourairports-data/airports.csv
Downloaded 2026-11-19. The game selects 35 airports per region and assigns
fictional destination shapes, capacities and demand. This is not flight data
for navigation. The original coordinate-only JSON was not available; the
catalogs were reconstructed from this dataset.

tools/build_map_data.py can regenerate the map resources from these inputs.
Python and Shapely are required only for regeneration, not to build or play.

Aircraft are now original vector silhouettes drawn by AirplanePainter, under
this project's MIT license. Their fill follows the airline colour and their
geometry scales with the map camera. Reusable SVG exports (two aircraft types
in eight colours) are in assets/aircraft; regenerate them with
python3 tools/export_aircraft.py. The former raster sprites are no longer used.
Maps are redrawn at the current screen scale, rather than stretching a bitmap.
The Americas retain North/South American land and nearby islands only.

Dependencies are resolved by Maven and keep their own licenses:
OpenJFX (GPLv2 with Classpath Exception), Jackson (Apache-2.0),
JUnit (EPL-2.0, test dependency).
